--file wants a file. Handed a directory it must fail rather than answer with
whatever reading a directory produces.

`.` is used as the directory because run.py copies only the FILES of a case
directory to the scratch directory, never a subdirectory, so a directory fixture
cannot be staged. The scratch directory itself is always there.

stderr is not pinned, for the reason given in cli-file-that-does-not-exist-fails:
the message is Foundation's, and localized.
